From: Robert Wojciechowski <24755+wojo@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] GLM on CUDA: a disk read sends each block's rows to the other ranks in one step, copied into place on the device

Upstream PR 1411 commit 1f6b50c67, the first of the two commits its head f0a9651b6 added after d730aff64, applied on
this package's 0001 and so on the recipe's engine series. f0a9651b6 is not carried: zig/src/native/cuda.zig is 600
lines on this series, not the 601 it was on upstream main, and 0001 already names the glm import at both places where
it compares, which is all that commit makes of the file, so its patch has no pre-image line here and nothing of it is
left to add.

What it does: a restore sent every piece of a block, each layer's rows for each other rank, through its own follow-link
step (a header and a payload broadcast, a header download, and a host copy of each page into that rank's lease), while
every decoding stream waited on rank 0's engine thread, and at TP=3 the shared rows crossed twice. Now a block's pieces
go as one table and one broadcast of their bytes (more steps only past the link's room), the shared rows once for every
rank, and each rank copies its pieces from the link's buffer device to device.

Tests: cuda_disk_ranks_test.zig gains two cases, rank 0's steps for every rank and a follower's copies of its own and
the shared pieces, and its bad-step table takes rows entries in place of the put one; both run under test-cuda-host.

Hand-settled hunks: none. Every hunk of every file applied as it stands: cuda_disk_ranks.zig, cuda_disk_image.zig and
cuda_glm_disk.zig matched the commit's pre-image byte for byte on this series, and the test file's first hunk took an
offset of one line over this package's 0011/0012 head flag in the test rig, which changes no context.

